@page "/modal"

<CatHead Title="Modal">
    A question that has to be answered before anything else happens.
    <code>.modal</code> is the panel; header, body and footer are three optional rows. You own the
    content and what the answer means.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-window-2-line"></i>
    <strong>Put it on a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> and open it with <code>showModal()</code>.</strong>
    The top layer, the focus trap, <kbd>Escape</kbd>, inert content behind and returning focus on
    close all come from the platform, and none of them are things a <code>div</code> overlay does
    without a lot of code that is usually subtly wrong. There is no
    <code>.modal-backdrop</code> involved: <code>::backdrop</code> is the platform's own, so the
    dimming is a pseudo-element rather than an element in the page.
    <code>sednaUi.confirm()</code> is built exactly this way.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Modal.DialogModal" Title="A real modal" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open it.</strong> <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes it, focus is trapped inside while
        it is open and returns to the button afterwards, and the page behind is inert. None of that
        is written here.
    </p>
    <p>
        The whole panel is wrapped in <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code>, which is what makes
        every button in it close the dialog with <strong>no JavaScript at all</strong> —
        <code>value</code> becomes <code>dialog.returnValue</code>, so “which button” is answered
        without a handler. Only opening needs a line of script.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing at the heading is worth the two attributes: a modal
        dialog is announced by its accessible name, and without one a screen reader says “dialog”
        and stops.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.ShowModalFromCsharp" Title="Opening one from C#" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open it.</strong> <code>ISednaUi.ShowModalAsync(id)</code> is the one line of
        script the example above says is needed, without the inline
        <code>onclick</code> and without injecting <code>IJSRuntime</code>. That matters more than
        it sounds: an app that would not inject <code>IJSRuntime</code> had no way to reach
        <code>showModal()</code>, so it fell back to a <code>.modal-backdrop</code> div behind an
        <code>@@if</code> — and lost the top layer, the focus trap, <kbd>Escape</kbd> and the inert
        background, all four at once.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>CloseModalAsync(id, returnValue)</code> is for the routes out that are <em>not</em> a
        <code>method="dialog"</code> submit; the platform handles those and <kbd>Escape</kbd>
        itself. An id that is not a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, or one already open, warns in the
        console and does nothing — an exception crossing the interop boundary from a Blazor handler
        tears down the circuit.
    </p>
    <p>
        Use <a href="/script"><code>ConfirmAsync</code></a> for a question with two answers. This
        is for a dialog holding a form.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Modal.ConfirmDialog" Title="The panel" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The three rows on their own, so they can be read without opening anything. This is the same
        markup as above with the <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> and the form taken off — a
        <code>.modal</code> is an ordinary bordered panel until something puts it in the top layer.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Modal.DestructiveConfirm" Title="Destructive confirm" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Name the consequence in the body, and make the confirming button the destructive one. Focus
        the <em>safe</em> choice when it opens: <code>showModal()</code> focuses the first focusable
        element, which is Cancel by source order here, and for a destructive action that ordering is
        the point rather than an accident.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Modal.ScrollingBody" Title="Scrolling body" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>The body scrolls on its own; there is nothing to add.</strong> The panel is capped
        at the viewport, the header and the footer keep their height, and
        <code>.modal-body</code> is the part that gives way — with the fading scroll edges, so a
        body carrying more below says so.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is not a nicety. A backdrop centres its panel, so a panel taller than the window grows
        past <em>both</em> edges: the header off the top and the footer — which is where the primary
        action is — off the bottom. A fourteen-field form was not submittable on a 900px window, and
        nothing on screen said why, because no element had a scrollbar.
    </p>
    <p>
        The cap is <code>--modal-max-height</code>, <code>calc(100dvh - 2 * var(--space-8))</code>
        by default and set to 240px here so the demo fits the page. <code>dvh</code> rather than
        <code>vh</code>: on a phone <code>vh</code> is measured with the address bar retracted, so a
        <code>vh</code> cap is taller than what is visible exactly where room is tightest.
    </p>
    <p>
        Wrapping header, body and footer in a <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> changes nothing — the form's
        box is removed with <code>display: contents</code> so the panel's flex column reaches
        through it. Without that the panel has one flex item and the body never becomes the part
        that scrolls.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>One thing is still markup:</strong> a body that scrolls and holds nothing focusable
        needs <code>tabindex="0"</code> with a <code>role</code> and a name, as this one has, so a
        keyboard reader can scroll it. A body of form fields already has focusable children and
        needs none of that.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Modal.Widths" Title="Widths" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.modal-sm</code> is 360px and <code>.modal-lg</code> is 760px, against the default
        480px. Width is the only thing they change — a yes/no question does not need the room a
        form does, and a diff or a table needs more. Pick by how wide the content has to be, not
        by how important the dialog is.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Modal.Deck" Title="A first-run deck" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A short sequence of slides in a modal, with progress dots and Back/Next. It is the other
        half of first-run: <a href="/spotlight">spotlight</a> anchors to the page, and before a
        reader has seen the page there is nothing to anchor to.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sequencing stays yours</strong>, exactly as it does for spotlight — which step is
        showing, what the buttons do, whether the deck has been seen before. Three things are here
        because each is wrong once before it is right: <code>--deck-height</code> fixes the body's
        height so the footer does not jump between slides; <code>margin: auto 0</code> centres a
        short slide but lets a tall one start at the top and scroll; and the entrance animation
        re-runs per step only if the step element is actually <em>replaced</em>, which in Blazor
        means keying it on the step number. Without the key the framework reuses the node and the
        deck goes still after slide one.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Make each dot a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> that jumps to its step.</strong> A reader
        who has understood slide one should not have to press Next three times to get back to where
        they were, and the dots are already the position indicator. A <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> is the
        report-only form; the drawing is identical either way, and the button form gets a pointer, a
        hover state, the library's focus ring and a 24&nbsp;×&nbsp;12&nbsp;px hit target around the
        6&nbsp;px circle. Mark the current one with <code>aria-current="step"</code> — on a button that
        replaces <code>.deck-dot--active</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Put the position in words as well — “3 of 5” in the footer, or an <code>aria-label</code>
        on the dots' container — because a row of circles is not announced as a position by anything.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Modal/BackdropForm.html" Title="Without a dialog">
    <p>
        <code>.modal-backdrop</code> dims and centres a panel inside the page instead. It is the
        fallback, for markup that cannot be a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> — you are already inside a
        form and cannot nest another, or the panel has to stay in the page's own stacking context.
    </p>
    <p>
        Then <code>role="dialog"</code> and <code>aria-modal="true"</code> are on you, and so is
        everything the platform was doing: <kbd>Escape</kbd>, keeping <kbd>Tab</kbd> inside the
        panel, making the rest of the page unreachable, and putting focus back where it came from.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-stack-line"></i>
    <strong>Z-order:</strong> the backdrop sits at 500, and a top-layer <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>
    ignores the scale entirely — the platform orders it by promotion. Anything you layer above a
    modal respects the shared chain: spotlight 510 &lt; popover and dropdown 550 &lt; toast 600 &lt;
    tips and the reconnect banner 1000. A <a href="/drawer">drawer</a> sits below at 480/490, so a
    modal opened from inside one still covers it. Do not invent a value; pick the rung you belong
    on — a test fails on anything else.
</div>
